\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The SICA pipeline. Both SC and SICA sample $K$ traces. SC selects the majority answer directly. SICA extracts FOL constraints from each trace, deduplicates, and solves a MAX-SAT instance via Z3. Dashed arrow: the oracle variant substitutes gold FOL annotations.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Constraint Extraction}
\label{sec:constraint-extraction}

For each trace $t_i$, the same LLM that generated $t_i$ is prompted to extract structured FOL constraints. While we prompt for FOL-style formalizations, the extracted constraints are predominantly propositional ground clauses without quantifiers, reflecting instruction-scale model capabilities. The extraction prompt requests JSON output containing the constraint type, a human-readable expression, and a Z3-compatible formula string. Each constraint $c$ is associated with its source trace $t_i$ and the answer $a_i$ that trace produced. The full prompt template is in Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}.

We define the \emph{extraction rate} as the fraction of traces yielding at least one parseable constraint. Traces with no valid constraints are excluded from MAX-SAT but still contribute answers to the candidate pool. Deduplication proceeds by syntactic equivalence: each unique constraint $c_m$ receives weight $w_m$ equal to the number of distinct traces that produced it.

\subsection{Weighted Partial MAX-SAT Formulation}
\label{sec:maxsat}

Let $\mathcal{A}(q) = \{a : a \in \{a_1, \ldots, a_K\}\}$ denote the set of distinct candidate answers for question $q$, and let $\mathcal{C} = \{c_1, \ldots, c_M\}$ denote the deduplicated constraint pool with weights $\{w_1, \ldots, w_M\}$.

\paragraph{MAX-SAT instance.} Each unique constraint $c_m$ serves as a soft clause with weight $w_m$. The objective is to find the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3's optimization engine solves this with a 10-second per-problem timeout.

\paragraph{Answer scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$ denote the excluded constraint set. Each candidate answer $a \in \mathcal{A}(q)$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) \;-\; \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) \in \{0, 1\}$ is a source-trace indicator: $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace that produced answer $a$, and 0 otherwise. The penalty weight $\alpha = 0.5$ in all experiments. The first term rewards answers whose source constraints are satisfied; the second penalizes answers whose source constraints were excluded by MAX-SAT. Discriminative signal arises when constraints from different traces conflict: Z3 excludes low-weight constraints from $\mathcal{C}^*$, and the scorer attributes these exclusions to the corresponding source answers via $\sigma$ (see Algorithm~\ref{alg:scorer} in Appendix~\ref{app:z3-pseudocode}). To ensure each candidate answer participates in scoring, the pipeline injects an \emph{answer assertion} constraint $\texttt{answer\_a} \mathrel{{=}{=}} \texttt{True}$ for each trace, with weight 1. This guarantees that every candidate receives a nonzero base score proportional to its vote count; SICA is therefore a strict generalization of SC. The selected answer is $a^* = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \text{score}(a)$.

\paragraph{Degeneration under non-discriminative constraints.}
In practice, self-extracted constraints are tightly coupled to their source answer: $c_m$ derived from trace $t_i$ typically entails $a_i$ ($\sigma(c_m, a_i) \approx 1$). When constraints lack discriminative power across candidates, MAX-SAT excludes almost no constraints ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), so the penalty term vanishes regardless of $\alpha$, and the score function degenerates:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
which reduces to a weighted count of traces supporting $a$, equivalent to majority voting when weights are uniform. Even direct per-answer Z3 evaluation of self-extracted constraints would yield the same outcome, since only 8.2\% of constraints are semantically discriminative across candidates (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). The ablation in \S\ref{sec:ablation} confirms this empirically: MAX-SAT, count-only, and random-weight baselines produce nearly identical accuracy.

\subsection{The $C_0$ Diagnostic}
\label{sec:c0}

We define the multi-candidate rate $C_0$ as the fraction of questions for which $K$ sampled traces produce more than one distinct answer:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:c0}
C_0 = \frac{|\{q \in \mathcal{Q} : |\mathcal{A}(q)| > 1\}|}{|\mathcal{Q}|}.
\end{equation}

$C_0$ is a zero-cost diagnostic computable before any constraint extraction. When $C_0$ falls below 5\%, nearly all questions are answered unanimously, and SICA produces the same output as SC by construction. High $C_0$ does not guarantee improvement, as it merely ensures disagreement exists without ensuring constraints can resolve it. $C_0$ serves as a necessary-condition screening criterion for practitioners considering symbolic aggregation.

\subsection{Z3 Feedback Refinement}
\label{sec:z3-feedback}

The standard SICA pipeline extracts constraints in a single open-loop pass, with no feedback from the solver to the extraction model. Z3 Feedback Refinement introduces a closed loop between the solver and the LLM, an instance of the LLM-Modulo paradigm \citep{kambhampati2024position} where an external verifier provides signals that the model cannot generate through introspection alone.

After initial constraint extraction (\S\ref{sec:constraint-extraction}), Z3 performs a consistency check on each trace's constraint set. When Z3 identifies an unsatisfiable subset, it returns the minimal UNSAT core: the smallest set of mutually contradictory constraints. A refinement prompt presents the UNSAT core alongside the original reasoning trace and asks the LLM to resolve the contradiction by revising or replacing the conflicting constraints. The refined constraints replace the originals, and the consistency check repeats for up to $N = 3$ rounds or until the constraint set is satisfiable. The final refined constraints enter the standard MAX-SAT scoring pipeline (\S\ref{sec:maxsat}).

The key distinction from standard self-extraction is the source of the corrective signal. In standard SICA, the LLM extracts constraints without any feedback on their logical coherence. In Z3 Feedback Refinement, the solver identifies specific contradictions that the LLM missed, providing an external verification signal. Whether this signal is sufficient to improve constraint discriminativeness is an empirical question (\S\ref{sec:interventions}).

\subsection{Scope and Limitations}
\label{sec:scope}

SICA as evaluated here uses \emph{same-model self-extraction}: the same LLM generates traces and extracts constraints. This isolates whether an LLM's own reasoning can be usefully formalized for aggregation. Published methods achieving positive results through different mechanisms \citep{xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, luo2025logicagent} are complementary to our findings, as each introduces capabilities beyond the reasoning model itself. An oracle variant (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) disentangles extraction quality from aggregation effectiveness. Beyond evaluating the standard pipeline, we test six remediation strategies that probe specific hypothesized causes of the capability trap (\S\ref{sec:interventions}): prompt-level (contrastive extraction), architectural (cross-model extraction, generational scaling), and solver-level (Z3 feedback refinement, consistency filtering, cross-trace UNSAT detection).

